Trial patch: give the CPU backend a Metal-style alloc-only buffer type, and
make the model loader actually use it for CPU-assigned layers.

Upstream (tc-mb llama.cpp-omni) supports "stripped" (-les) MoE models whose
routed expert tensors are absent from the GGUF and must be synthesized at
load time into an alloc-only buffer (real storage is streamed in later by
the slot-fill machinery). llama-model.cpp asks the backend registry for a
proc named "ggml_backend_dev_buffer_type_alloc_only" (make_alloc_only_buft).
At the pinned commit the only implementation is Metal's; the CPU backend
answers nothing, so loading a stripped model on a CPU-only device dies at
the first routed expert tensor:

    create_expert_tensor_from_manifest: backend has no alloc-only buft,
    cannot synthesize stripped expert tensor 'blk.0.ffn_down_exps.weight'

Part 1 mirrors the Metal semantics for CPU:
  - ggml/include/ggml-backend.h: declare ggml_backend_cpu_alloc_only_buffer_type().
  - ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp: define a "CPU_AllocOnly" buffer type whose
    buffers really allocate memory (CPU buffers are directly dereferenceable,
    so unlike the GPU alloc-only type this is a plain allocating buft with a
    distinct identity) and reuse the default CPU interface / host free path.
  - ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.cpp: answer the proc-address lookup for
    "ggml_backend_dev_buffer_type_alloc_only" with a wrapper that maps the
    device index the loader passes (0 == the single CPU device) to the
    CPU_AllocOnly buffer type.

Part 1 alone is not enough: the loader never queries the CPU device.
  - src/llama-model.cpp (load_tensors): the alloc_only_buft map is only
    populated for devices in the model's device list, and that list never
    contains the CPU device (llama_prepare_model_devices keeps only
    GPU/IGPU/RPC devices; CPU layers are the implicit fallback). Ask for
    the CPU device's alloc-only buft explicitly next to the existing
    cpu_dev lookup.
  - src/llama-model.cpp (create_tensor_own_alloc,
    create_expert_tensor_from_manifest): derive the lookup device from the
    layer's assigned device (dev_layer[bid].dev) instead of from the
    layer's first buffer type. The CPU backend's default buft reports a
    NULL device (upstream FIXME in ggml-backend.cpp), so the old
    derivation yields nullptr for any layer whose buft list starts with
    the plain CPU buft, and the map lookup misses even with the entry
    present. On GPU paths the two derivations agree.

